---
title: More on invariants
date: 2026-09-13
---
My dissertation is called [invariants](https://github.com/ujaandas/invariants), and the short version is: it's a
compiler for making LLMs stop generating numbers that don't add up.

### The actual problem

Ask a model for JSON with a `unit_price`, a `quantity`, and a `total_price`, and every existing structured-output tool -
grammars, JSON Schema, whatever - can guarantee you get three numbers in the right shape. None of them can guarantee
`total_price` actually equals `unit_price * quantity`. That's not a syntax problem, it's a semantics problem, and
grammars only know about syntax. So in practice, everyone just validates after the fact and regenerates when it's wrong,
which is slow, and still doesn't actually *prevent* the bad output - it just catches it after the model already wasted a
forward pass being wrong.

### The idea

Treat it as a compiler problem instead of a validation problem.

You write a schema in a small DSL I built - fields, types, and invariants, like:

```
spec CloudProvision {
    field base_ram: Integer { value >= 8; }
    field instance_count: Integer { value IN [1, 2, 4, 8]; }
    field total_ram: Integer { }

    invariant calc_ram {
        this.total_ram == this.base_ram * this.instance_count;
    }
}
```

That gets compiled ahead of time - lexed, parsed, semantically bound, and then run through a dependency analysis pass
that builds a graph over the fields and topologically sorts it into an execution schedule. `total_ram` depends on
`base_ram` and `instance_count`, so it gets scheduled after both, and because the compiler can see it's a closed-form
equality, it gets flagged as *deterministically computable* - meaning the model is never asked to generate it at all.
The runtime just computes it.

For everything the model does have to generate, the runtime masks its logits token by token: every candidate token gets
checked against the field's constraints (in progress, not just once it's finished) before it's even allowed to be
sampled. So a `value >= 8` field literally cannot produce a `7`, not because something rejected it afterwards, but
because `7` was never a legal next token to begin with.

### What went wrong

Every one of the actually hard bugs showed up at the boundary between "compiled schedule" and "a live, non-deterministic
model," and most of them are pretty funny in hindsight:

- My original binder registered a field and bound its constraints in the same pass, which meant a field referencing
another field declared *later* in the same spec just silently failed to resolve. Fix was a two-pass binder - register
every signature first, then bind constraints once everything's visible.
- Dependency tracking originally identified fields by an integer id scoped to their own symbol table, which broke the
second a spec could be nested or reused twice in the same document - same id, two different actual fields. Had to switch
to fully-qualified string paths instead.
- BPE and SentencePiece tokenizers mark word boundaries with completely different characters, which quietly broke my
structural validity checks until I handled both explicitly. This is also the entire reason I benchmarked against two
separate model families instead of one - a single tokenizer would never have caught it.
- For a while, only the top 1,000 highest-probability tokens ever got checked against the mask, for speed. Anything
ranked lower kept its original score completely untouched - meaning a model that really wanted an invalid token could
just reach past the window and grab it. Nobody was stopping it.
- A non-contiguous NumPy array got silently *copied* by pybind11 instead of mutated in place, so for a while the model
was running on completely unmasked logits with zero errors raised anywhere. I only caught this because a
heavily-constrained field produced a value that should've been impossible.
- `llama.cpp`'s end-of-sequence token decodes to an empty string, which slid straight through my masking loop looking
like a harmless no-op and quietly ended generation early.
- A numeric bound like `<= 80` was being checked digit-by-digit, but a value like `160` only got rejected once it had
*already finished generating* - which defeats the entire point. Took a genuinely annoying amount of thinking about
monotonicity (appending a digit to a non-negative number can only make it bigger) to prune those early instead of
after the fact. None of these got caught by unit tests. All of them got caught by actually running the thing against
a live model and watching it do something it shouldn't have been able to do. 
    
    
### Does it actually work 

Benchmarked across twelve suites (some synthetic, some pulled from real JSON Schemas - Stripe invoices, Kubernetes resource
limits, GitHub Actions configs) against two model families and four other systems (a plain unconstrained prompt,
`llama.cpp`'s own grammar, and two third-party libraries, Outlines and Guidance): 
- 90% of field-level assertions pass, against 84% (Guidance), 80% (grammar baseline), 75% (Outlines), and 1% for a plain prompt with no constraints
at all. 
- On constraints that are pure cross-field math - the entire reason this project exists - every
grammar-based system craters to ~54-59%, while invariants holds at 88.7%. Makes sense: that's the one category a
context-free grammar was never going to be able to express in the first place. 
- It's also ~22% faster on average than the grammar baseline, mostly because a field that gets computed instead of generated costs zero tokens. The one
place it loses is long, totally unconstrained free-text fields, where there's nothing to skip and the
full-vocabulary mask has to run on every single token for no benefit - the overhead has nothing to offset it. Good
to know your failure mode, at least. 

The only real "big" problem that I still haven't quite solved is that nothing generated can ever be un-generated, if a value turns out to violate a constraint discovered later, the only option is to crash. No backtracking. Solving that would require speculative generation with rollback (which I actually wanted to do, just didn't have the time/scope to do so).

Anyway - this thing is basically a small language with a real frontend, binder, and dependency-aware
scheduler bolted onto an LLM's sampling loop, and I think each of those phases is interesting enough to talk about
on its own. So that's next.